From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DenialOfGravity <DenialOfGravity@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 18 Nov 2026 23:44:23 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] redfin 1258: kern.sweet_surf_inuse: IOSurfaceIsInUse as on an
 iPhone (use counts + per-submission GPU holds)

0983 published state page +0x18 (what IOSurfaceIsInUse reads: ldr x8,[client+0x70]; ldr x8,[x8,#0x18]; != 0) only
for scanout slots, so every other surface read "not in use" and pools (CVPixelBufferPool, CA tile/backing pools,
decoders) recycled surfaces the GPU or display still used: Photos' zoomed image a mosaic of repeated tiles, camera
preview jumping back in time. Based on the unmerged 1048 (ws-cam-inuse cfa79a2), adapted to the NativeAdreno ABI.

kern.sweet_surf_inuse 0..15 (boot-arg sweetsurfinuse sets the start value). 0 (default) = 0983 exactly.
Non-zero: every surface publishes clientUse + displayUse + gpuHold (category 0) and clientUse1 (category 1).
- IOSurfaceIncrement/DecrementUseCount (traps) count for every surface; a client's own counts go with its
  reference (exit/crash), as MiniIOSurface's dropRef.
- GPU holds: NativeAdrenoAbiSubmit flags bit 1 (NativeAdrenoAbiSubmitBoListFlag, new) = a BO list follows the
  buffers ({count, 0} + {handle, flags bit0 write, 0} entries). Exactly the IOSurfaces behind the named imports
  (matched once per import by byte 0's physical address) are held; a submission without the list (older userland)
  holds every surface its client has imported and mapped. Holds are taken before the job is queued (fence pending),
  given the fence after (cancelled if the submission fails), dropped when the fence lands (GPU IRQ kick, 5 ms poll
  fallback, 2 s cap); a GPU reset completes every fence and kicks; a freed client kicks after its last-fence wait.
- +2: "SWEET SURF INUSE: 5s: ..." klog line. +4: no GPU holds. +8: IOSurfaceLock waits (<= 200 ms) for the newest
  GPU job that writes the surface (read-only lock) or uses it (read-write lock), as IOSurfaceLock syncs with the
  GPU on iOS. One GPU ring: GPU-to-GPU use across processes is ordered already (no wait needed).
